A||N||17||85Josh||Oil in my Air Filter!(Duke)||||4||
Z||000000||85Josh||04-06-2001||05:57 PM||||Somehow, I am getting oil into the air filter housing on my 85 Iron Duke <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I have already replaced the valve cover PCV system grommets, the oil/air seperator, and the PCV valve. I also cleaned everything in the system, and replaced the grommet on the bottom of the housing itself, the one that the seperator goes into. What else could cause this? I'm losing about 6-8 tablespoons of oil every week. TIA<BR><P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||4
Z||000001||Indyellowgt||04-06-2001||06:21 PM||||Oh dear....<BR>Sounds like the 'ol DUKE lives up to its reputation once again!!<BR>Sounds like worn rings..Eventually loss or compression,and...clunk.<BR>Sorry the bad news may kill your weekend...<P><BR>Mark   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000002||85Josh||04-06-2001||06:28 PM||||Guess I'd better get my compression gauge back from my friend and check!||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||04-06-2001||07:28 PM||||yep, sucking crankcase oil into the system.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-06-2001||07:39 PM||||Quick test.<P>Pull the air cleaner and start the car. (leave the PCV filter on the valve cover.)<P>at idle cover the breather opening and see if pressure builds up.<P>Then bring the RPM up and see if pressure builds.<P>much easier than compression testing. <P>Usually at idle most cars seem to slowly  develope a vacuum at the breather or at least keep the same pressure.<P>If the problem is just starting it will show up more at elevated RPM.<P>Could be rings...<BR>Could also be exhaust vavle seals/guides...<BR>Could be a combination of rings and exhaust valve problems.<P>If pressure builds fast it's probably rings...<P>Also,,, try just replacing the filter. If the filter is old it could be holding allot of crap and adding to the problem.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||85Josh||04-06-2001||08:25 PM||||Thanks for the replies, guys, that's about what I was fearing. I will try your test, ogre, I can't remember who I loaned my compression tester to!||||reg||1
Z||000006||85Josh||04-06-2001||08:38 PM||||Ok, back inside now. theogre, it doesn't seem to be building any noticeable pressure or vacuum, but I can see oil vapor blowing out when it isn't covered. Also, what filter are you referring to? I changed both the airfilter and the breather/separator dealie about 3k miles ago, when I got the car. That was about 6 weeks ago, I drive a LOT, LOL! BTW, she's got 150k on the clock, and still has a lot of fun in her.<P>Hey, spellcheck works!<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)<P>[This message has been edited by 85Josh (edited 04-06-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by 85Josh (edited 04-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||LPR||04-06-2001||09:43 PM||||You don't want to hear this... but I had the same thing happen to my 84 a year after I got it.  It got really bad right up to the end of its life  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">, the compression in the cylinders was an average of 87! ONE WAS 10! I replaced the engine and she perrrrrs like a kitten now!  I thought the car was powerful before!!!! WOWIE! (A good engine makes a world of difference!)<BR>I'm sorry about this, I hope it doesn't bring you down!<BR>LUC||||reg||8
