Z||000009||85Josh||04-06-2001||09:58 PM||||LPR, nothing gets me down, its all in how you look at it! Maybe I'm fated to be driving a Caddy 4.9 or a Buick 3800!||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||04-06-2001||11:39 PM||||Josh,<P>Afterthought<BR>Duh, almost forgot the obvivous... did you try replacing the PCV valve?<BR>end afterthought...<P><BR>I ment the breather for the valve cover, but you alreay did that.<P>Blowing vapor means the engine is getting pretty worn. How much longer it will last only god knows. My caravan is blowing heavily but is still runs like a scalded bat most of the time. I'll keep runnning it untill it fries then go find another just like it. (Well... maybe a little nicer interior...) Yours isn't building pressure at idle so that's a good sign. means it's in the earlier stages.<P>easiest thing to do is line up another motor and have it ready to drop in when this one goes belly up. That gives you time to consider a V6/V8 upgrade or simply using a rebuilt 4 that's had basic things like a port and polish done to it.<P>I drove an 84 4/stick awhile back. was going to buy it untill some dumb s__t hit the fan. I like the 4's. (The one I have is an 87/auto. the 84 would have been #2.) Bloody hard to beat the 4 for a daily driver car.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||85Josh||04-07-2001||09:41 PM||||theogre, I did replace the PCV valve about 3k miles ago, too. I got it from the dealer, and I am fairly sure it is the correct one.<P>I think my best solution is to keep cleaning out the air filter housing until she grenades, and get another engine from 007DOUG or someone to go through in the meantime. Thanks!<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000012||kinboyatuwo||04-07-2001||11:30 PM||||Maybee good news!<P>mine was doing that about 5 months ago<BR>running great and stopped after a month or so,not sure why it stopped but it did<P>did an engine flush and changed to synthetic not sure if that is why though<P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||7
Z||000013||rogergarrison||04-08-2001||01:10 AM||||mine used a little oil qt like 600-700 miles when i put it in. I just added K&N filter and Synthetic oil and it stopped completely. I drive a ton, so I dont change till over 6 or 7 thou miles. I do 90% hiway miles.||||reg||1
Z||000014||85Josh||04-08-2001||05:39 AM||||kinboyatwo, we can only hope!<P>rogergarrison, that pretty much describes my driving style, too. <P>It looks like I'd better switch to synthetic, and maybe the K&N, too.||||reg||1
Z||000015||1988LRC||04-08-2001||12:26 PM||||try Rotella||||reg||1
Z||000016||Tigger||04-08-2001||11:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by kinboyatuwo:<BR><B>did an engine flush and changed to synthetic not sure if that is why though</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I get a slight amount of oil in my air cleaner every once in a while and have been thinking about it since I first read this thread.  My assumption on all this is that the oil passages that drain back to the crankcase could be somewhat plugged and not allowing the oil to drain down.  I notice the oil usually after running high rpms too.  Makes sense to me now that the oil is probably building up in the valve cover.  This spring I'll do a flush when I change oil.  I need to check compression as well, but the duke seems to be going strong.<P>kinboyatuwo, I'd say the flush is what solved the problem.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||85Josh||04-09-2001||06:37 PM||||Tigger, that sounds like a possibility. When I first got this car, I used the $1.99 oil flush in a can, and ran 15w-40 oil, since I hadn't read the owners manual.I cleaned the old oil out of the airfilter housing at htat time,and I seem to remember that the housing was still clean when I changed the oil to 5W-30 two weeks later.||||reg||1
